On Tue, 21 Feb 2006, alexxx.magni@gmail.com wrote:

> What I don't understand: scrolls.
> I find one, and it says "scroll named QQWEWQ". What should I do? I read
> it!

That's bad. You should try to find out the effects of scrolls before
reading them. In particular, the cheapest scrolls are scrolls of identify;
by comparing prices in shops, you can single them out. Then, read those
scrolls to identify the other scrolls.

(Of course, there are much more details in the object identification
spoiler and the scroll spoiler.)

> Then something happens (various effects for now: my blade turning blue,
> my hawaian t-shirt glowing etc..) I guess those are descriptions of
> magic I cannot comprehend, not having ID-ed the scroll.
> My question: AFTER this happens, it asks me => name the scroll
> as:......
> when I write down something like "t-shirt colorizer" nothing else
> happen, and I do not have any scroll, anymore.
> So?
> I can understand that it vanished since I used it, but what's the use
> of naming a thing already disappeared?

In NetHack, there is a difference between naming things and naming classes
of things. You can name two bags "bag with escape items" and "bag with
stuff": This is naming of individual items. Calling a class of items means
that all such items will be identified to you in this particular well: In
your example, if you come across a scroll of the same kind, it will be
identified to you as "a scroll called t-shirt colorizer".

To play around with namind and calling, use the 'N' command.

